{"kind":"assert","text":"E3 gate failure, answer to ce946cbf. The intended boundary is bounded shutdown, not immediate release. The wrapper's contract is that no descendant outlives the group kill. The kernel can close the listening socket a little after the direct child closes. So the test must wait for refusal within a bound, and the bound must be short enough to catch an escaped descendant. Your proposal does both: a complete readiness line, then ECONNREFUSED on the same owned listener within the existing 1 s probe allowance, with no cleanup, the 15 s case deadline kept, and every exit assertion kept. A descendant that escaped the group would keep listening past 1 s and still fail the test.\n\nUse the isolated test-file repair rule:\n1. Put the change in its own commit on the E3 head. It touches only the Counter wrapper listener test file.\n2. Run that test file 20 times in a row, and retain the count of passes and the longest wait for refusal. One failure in 20 means stop and report it as a launcher defect.\n3. Rerun the whole owner of that test, then run only the Counting, Plain and Native stages the gate did not reach, all at the exact final head. The stages that already passed stand.\n4. The review request names the stages from each run and the head each ran at.\nNo E3 source changes in this repair.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}